ef at the  company they all work for lumen  industries he's taking over the role for  his best friend petey who he learns is  no longer with the company they work on  the severed floor which means that they  agreed to have a surgical procedure that  separates their work consciousness from  the one they have when they're outside  the office heli explains this to herself  in a video saying she gave consent for  her perceptual chronologies to be  surgically split separating her memories  between her work life and her personal  life the memories are spatially dictated  they're unable to access outside  recollections in the office and can't  retain work memories after they leave  she acknowledges she understands the  process is comprehensive and  irreversible you see how this works when  mark comes into the office at the  beginning of the episode his audi the  person he is when he's not at work is  upset to the point of crying before he  enters the building as he takes the  elevator down to the severed floor you  see him change over to his any  personality there's this great sequence  where he's walking down this long  sterile hallway you see that he throws  away the tissue that audi mark was  wiping his tears with inside the car and  it's pretty clear that the innie has no  recollection of what was bothering him  to the show's credit it does a pretty  good job of making this all seem  workable the way that they introduce  things to get you thinking about them  and then coming back to show the same  situation from the other perspective  gives you the sense that they've really  thought things through it's a pretty  great premise that's also a little  tricky if you were so inclined you could  probably poke a lot of holes in it but i  thought they did a good job in how they  presented it of course we meet his  severed co-workers irv has been there  the longest and he can remember a time  when they incentivize their good work  with coffee creamer like a lot of the  details you pick up it's hard to say if  that's something that's important or not  dylan seems like a go-getter that has  this super entertaining cynical side and  he's out to win the holy grail of the  current incentives the waffle party  that's awarded to the top refiner of the  quarter  you also meet his bosses who appear to  not be severed miltek oversees their  work in the macro data refinement  department and as their boss he has  information about their inside and their  outside lives the fact that he leads  heli through the onboarding process he's  even there when she has her procedure  done and he's the first person she sees  when she tries to leave through the  stairwell that was enough for me to  suppose he's not severed but there are  also a couple of articles i've read with  the actor where he confirms that i  imagine this is the case for their boss  harmony coble as well i'm not saying  there isn't anything weird going on with  these two but would they really be able  to do their jobs if they had the same  process done to them ms koble is also  audi mark's neighbor mrs selvig and she  appears to employ two different  personalities in relating with him as a  strategy at work she's distant and  generally a tough boss and outside she's  warm and friendly this is emphasized in  her two stories about her mother at work  she describes her as an atheist who told  her that hell is just a product of a  morbid imagination but that whatever  humans can imagine they can usually  create at home she describes her as a  catholic who said it takes the saints  eight hours to bless a sleeping child  and she hopes mark isn't rushing them in  both cases she's trying to get him to be  a better employee and it'll be  interesting to see how engaging both  sides of him plays out what they  actually do during their work hours is a  big mystery you see it consists of  looking at screens full of numbers and  separating the scary looking ones into  different bins this is how they explain  it to heli anyway who doesn't get it at  all at first but she's able to see it by  the end of the second episode when he's  asked about working at lumen in the  outside world the woman asks if he  designs the medicines and then someone  interjects that they thought lumen was  more on the tech side of things on the  package for the microchip they implant  in their brains the company name reads  lumen medical technology it's also  mentioned that it's been around since  the 1800s started out making topical  solves and that's followed up with the  question what don't they make overall it  sounds like a conglomerate or a mega  corporation and it's not clear if their  logo that resembles a droplet is a major  clue towards their history or what the  severed workers do specifically mark  doesn't exactly take to his new role as  the head of the department even though  he tells heli who answered unknown for  four out of the five questions that she  got a perfect score she just isn't  taking to the orientation process she  even throws the speaker at his head  which gives him an injury and that earns  him a gift card at the end of the day it  does seem important that the note gives  a fictitious account of what happened  since it goes to show that they could  tell their audies anything about what  goes on at work and they wouldn't know  the difference to this point audi mark  is a little less interesting than what's  happening to his innie at work he lost  his wife in a car accident and we aren't  given all the details but this is  something he clearly hasn't gotten over  this is probably why he was willing to  shut his brain off for eight hours a day  through the severance procedure and at  home he drinks a lot which perhaps  that's him trying to shut it down there  as well he used to be a history  professor he has a sister who he's close  with even though he might not be a great  match for her husband and their  pretentious friends as witnessed at the  no dinner dinner party the most  intriguing thing that happens outside  the office is that petey interrupts his  vip dinner at pips to tell him that he's  been unsevered mark doesn't know who he  is but he does take the card he gives  him and that starts the ball rolling for  him wanting to know more about what  lumen's actually doing to them in the  second episode after he has a bad date  that includes a run-in with protesters  from the whole mine collective who are a  local group against the severance  process plus a visit from ms selvig who  brings over some cookies audi mark calls  out of work and does go looking for  petey the address on the back of his  card leads him to an abandoned  greenhouse where he finds his  ex-colleague who still isn't sure what  they do at work either being unsevered  hasn't made that any clearer but he is  sure that something creepy goes down in  the break room and he has a tape to  prove it earlier in the episode mark  took the blame for heli tripping the  code detector in the elevator and that  landed him in the break room of course  his audi has no recollection of that  happening petey is also trying to draw a  map of the severed floor and tells mark  that he left the original at work for  him to find after their conversation  mark decides to bring petey home and let  him stay in his extra room he is  suffering from reintegration sickness  which doesn't look fun and when he gets  a phone call that causes him to cough up  blood  in episode two we also get a look at  audi helly having her procedure done  what stands out about this whole  sequence is that she seems enthusiastic  about it and that it does appear to be  somewhat routine irving's been there for  three years so this is something they've  been doing even though we don't know how  many people have actually had it done  and it seems apparent that there's a lot  more than just the people that we've  seen so far considering there's talk of  legislation and that people are  protesting this idea you see that the  next thing helly's audi experiences  after going under is the stairwell  incident and you see that from the other  side it turns out milicek was there to  help turn her around because even though  her innie is fighting all the way her  audi does actually want to be there  because she's the audience surrogate in  the equation she has a ton of questions  about all the different aspects of their  jobs there are not a lot of straight  answers though there are a lot of  details that fall on a spectrum from  funny to possibly incredibly important  and that makes it hard to parse what you  should actually pay attention to her  work self does want to quit and no one  else in the office thinks that she'll  have much luck with that the last big  thing that happens is herb nods off at  his desk he pictures black sludge  pouring into his cubicle which is also  something that features heavily in the  great-looking title sequence that we  first saw at the beginning of this same  episode for his infraction he gets sent  to a wellness check where ms casey tells  him tidbits about his audi in an almost  hypnotic way he does get warned not to  react to any of the facts because he's  supposed to enjoy them all equally  without preference this does seem to be  soothing to irv's any even though it's  all fairly strange and i do think it's  interesting to note the different  non-severed people that they encounter  outside her office irv does meet another  severed worker named burt the head of  optics and design they share an  appreciation for a painting of the  company's founder kier egan swinging a  whip at a goat wearing a robe who has a  jester and a couple of women standing  behind him burt finds it calming and it  seems like these two have formed a  connection  so there are a lot of mysteries to  speculate about i really appreciated the  design of things the tech the long  hallways the artwork the handbook  illustrations and the flow of the  training materials it's all very sterile  but still manages to look stylish or at  least stylized the cast is amazing and  it's hard to pick out a favorite  performance because they all seem vital  i feel like each of the characters  delivered more than one memorable moment  already and we're just getting started  this is absolutely an abusive situation  where the victims have to ponder what  drove them to their decision daily while  also having this experience that doesn't  truly feel like it ever ends everyone  who works in the basement subjected  their innie to a helpless existence one  that they presume to be irreversible and  they did that willingly i wondered about  when this was supposed to take place and  found an interview with the creator dan  erickson where he said it's not a far  off future it's around now it's like  vaguely now as he said we're not going  for something where this is 10 years in  the future where severance has been  invented and already exists it's sort of  an alternate vaguely nourished timeline  so they had an intentional plan to keep  a lot of ambiguity to the time and place  and as far as the technology is  concerned they also intentionally never  fully decided how things worked even for  themselves  for me this adds to the uneasiness of  everything and i think it sounds like a  good choice to make it about the  characters and what they're experiencing  more than specific details that's part  of why i want to hold off to see where  things go before i get too attached to  any speculation about what's happening  i've read some different theories some  of those are pretty well thought out  about what the numbers mean and even  some where people think that what they  see is different than what we see but  i'm guessing we'll get more information  each week and at that pace it might be a  while before things come into focus it's  also possible for the reasons i  mentioned in relation to the choices  they've made that this will remain vague  even at the end of the s